# Build the responsive 16:9 split shell

## Scope and existing evidence

Historical evidence below describes current implementation limits. The
accepted finite contract may be theoretical and does not implement them.

Show both editors concurrently with adjustable division, stable selection,
keyboard navigation, zoom, page list, diagnostics, and no hidden main surface.

Current frontend evidence: both editors are visible in one 16:9 workspace. The
splitter supports pointer and keyboard adjustment from 35% through 65% without
forcing horizontal grid overflow, including at a 1024 px viewport, and exposes
a wider invisible pointer target than its visual rule. Preview zoom remains
local from 60% through 160%, with layout-aware page geometry keeping every edge
scroll-reachable at narrow, short, and wide browser sizes.

At 480 px and below, both panels stay visible at a fixed 50/50 split so the
document reflows without horizontal page scrolling; wider viewports restore the
adjustable 35-65% range. The workspace is viewport-bounded, with source and
preview overflow contained by their own scroll surfaces. At 320 by 480 px, the
Task field and page stage are both visible in the initial frame.

Very-short reflow keeps both Task and the page stage visible down to 225 px of
viewport height without document overflow. A checked-in 32-case Firefox matrix
crosses 320, 480, 481, and 1024 px widths with 225, 360, 480, and 576 px
heights, plus both split and 60%/160% zoom extremes. Every state keeps the
document and workspace viewport-bounded, both editor surfaces visible, and all
four page edges scroll-reachable.

A checked-in JavaScript-disabled Firefox fixture keeps the short-height
warning visible without taking workspace flow and observes only document and
stylesheet requests. BiDi viewport emulation at 320 and 481 pixels confirms the
static shell remains 50/50 with an inert divider, visible Task and page-stage
surfaces, and no document overflow before `main.js` is available.

The task stays open for backend-fed page navigation and stable semantic
selection.

## Finite dual-surface layout and navigation contract

**Inputs:** viewport width and height, a current accepted notebook revision,
selected semantic object and page, an optional pointer/keyboard divider
command, and a preview zoom command. **Outputs:** two concurrent editor
surfaces, one consistent selected identity and page focus, a reachable page
list, a diagnostics region, and bounded scroll/zoom state. Layout changes do
not alter notebook text, page geometry, or accepted revision.

**Desktop layout:** above 480 CSS px, use a 16:9-inspired split workspace with
source/LLM editor on the left and human page preview on the right. Both stay
visible. An operable divider is initially 50% and clamps to 35%-65%; a
keyboard-focusable separator supports left/right arrow steps of 5 percentage
points, announces its current value, and does not consume normal editor arrow
keys while focus remains in a text field.

Each panel owns its content scroll,
so reducing width must not create top-level document overflow. Page list and
diagnostics remain independently discoverable rather than covering a panel.

**Compact layout:** at or below 480 CSS px, stack the same two editors within
the viewport: task/source above, preview below, both visible as bounded
independently scrolling regions. The divider ceases to be draggable and is
not presented as an operable separator. Page list and diagnostics remain
accessible through labeled regions without replacing either editor. A compact
stack is a theoretical target, not a description of the current 50/50
side-by-side narrow implementation.

**Navigation and zoom:** page-list selection changes preview focus and
requests the matching stable semantic owner in source, without losing unsaved
source drafts. Diagnostics activate the exact owning object and accepted
revision; a missing/stale owner produces a visible non-destructive refusal
instead of selecting a similarly numbered object. Preview zoom clamps to
60%-160%, keeps all four page edges scroll-reachable, and never changes
physical page size or source identity. Keyboard Tab order exposes both editors,
page navigation, diagnostics and zoom without requiring a pointer.

**Worked example:** at 1024 x 576 CSS px, a 50/50 split exposes both editors;
pressing Right Arrow twice on the focused separator requests 60/40 while the
selected object `block-7` remains selected. At 320 x 480 CSS px the same
revision uses vertically bounded top/bottom editor regions, each scrollable;
selecting page 2 still focuses `block-7` when its accepted owner is on page 2.
At either size, 160% zoom may create preview scroll but no document overflow.

**Refusal and limits:** a page absent from the accepted notebook, stale
revision, unavailable source mapping, or non-finite zoom command leaves
selection and notebook unchanged and reports a visible diagnostic. The
existing frontend has working 35%-65% desktop resizing, a narrow fixed
side-by-side split and local preview zoom; it does **not** implement the
compact stack, backend-owned page list or semantic cross-panel selection.
Firefox viewport fixtures are evidence about the existing shell, not proof
of the future stacked layout. No cross-browser or assistive-technology
certification follows from this design.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** practical for the existing bounded desktop split and local
  scroll/zoom; theoretical for stacked compact behavior and backend-linked
  navigation, 2026-11-02.
- **Delivered:** finite desktop/compact layouts, keyboard separator contract,
  retained two-surface visibility, example viewport and selection behavior,
  explicit stale-target refusals and usability boundaries.
- **Not implemented by this acceptance:** compact stack, backend page-list
  navigation, stable semantic cross-panel focus, and certified accessibility.
